Elon, I think you're brilliant, and I admire so much of what you do. But I don’t understand your obsession with building a city on Mars. Even setting aside the challenges and cost of getting there and building the city, who would want to make the trip, let alone live there?  The long journey itself would expose people to elevated radiation levels and prolonged weightlessness. As you know, Mars has a very thin, non-breathable atmosphere, frigid temperatures, recurring planet-wide dust storms, and no Earth-like global magnetic field. The city you want to build would require heavily shielded, pressurized habitats, most likely underground.  Plus, even if you can overcome the difficulties of launching spaceships back to Earth during that two-week window every 26 months, returning settlers would face serious health problems from radiation exposure and prolonged weightlessness. Plus, after living for so long in just 38% of Earth’s gravity, they would also have considerable difficulty readapting to life on Earth.

@MarkJam73395966 @elonmusk If that is the case the moon is a far better alternative. Actually, an under ground city on earth makes a lot more sense than one on Mars.

@he1n_1080 @elonmusk Maybe, maybe not. But who would want to live there now, just in case that happens at some distant point in the future?

